    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Hillside once featured a mile-long Main Street with fifteen saloons that served as social hubs for miners and settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/hillside-south-dakota-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Diverse immigrant groups from Norway, Germany, and Russia helped shape the early community of Hillside.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/hillside-south-dakota-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Chicago, Burlington, and Quincy Railroad arrived in 1893, bringing new opportunities for trade and growth to Hillside.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/hillside-south-dakota-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mining operations drove the economy of Hillside until the Great Depression caused a mass exodus and eventual abandonment.",
